- a fanned tail, rounded body with shaded colour, lighter belly and cheek patch;
- a two-part beak and a bright eye;
- layered wings with feather tips that beat continuously.

There are three of them: a teal Indian Roller style, a golden oriole and a rosy starling with a black head. Instead of tumbling round in a circle, they now fly in spread-out arcs over the mic and tilt into their turns like real birds. The chirp marks and chirp sound stay in time with them.

I checked stills across the moment: the three birds stay apart and clear of the headline, and both checks pass.

